## 3. 📝 Corporate booking has no working frontend

**Location:** `src/app/schedule/page.tsx` (and absence of any `corporate`
route under `src/app/`)

**Cause:** `schedule/page.tsx` only ever calls `trpc.bookings.book`, the
personal-membership booking procedure. `corporateBookings.book` is fully
implemented server-side but nothing in the UI calls it.

**Impact:** A member linked to a company (e.g. Rahul, linked to TechCorp)
can only ever book using their personal membership credits through the
app as it stands. TechCorp's credit pool balance stays at 250 regardless
of member activity; "Recent Corporate Bookings" always shows "No bookings
yet."

**Decision: documented, not fixed.** Building a working corporate booking
flow is a real feature addition (a new page or a toggle on the existing
schedule page), not a bug fix — out of scope given the remaining time.

---

## 4. 📝 Three of four notification types are never created

**Location:** `src/server/routers/notifications.ts`

**Cause:** The schema defines four notification types
(`waitlist_promotion`, `class_cancelled`, `membership_expiring`,
`announcement`), but this router only ever inserts `"announcement"` rows,
via `broadcast`. No code anywhere inserts the other three.

**Impact:** A member promoted from a waitlist, or affected by a class
cancellation, receives no notification — even though the schema and (very
likely) the UI expect one.

**Decision: documented, not fixed.** Wiring up `waitlist_promotion` and
`class_cancelled` inserts would naturally belong alongside fixes #6 and #7
below, but implementing and re-testing three new notification paths
wasn't feasible in the remaining time. `membership_expiring` specifically
would require a scheduled job, which doesn't exist anywhere else in this
codebase — a larger architectural addition than this scope should include.

---

## 5. 📝 Admin broadcast excludes trainers and admins

**Location:** `src/server/routers/notifications.ts`, `broadcast` procedure

**Cause:**
```ts
const activeMembers = await ctx.db.select({ id: users.id })
  .from(users).where(eq(users.role, "member"));
```
This filters strictly to `role === "member"`, so trainers and admins never
receive broadcast announcements.

**Impact:** A gym-wide announcement (e.g. "closed for maintenance") never
reaches staff.

**Decision: documented, not fixed.** Genuinely ambiguous whether this is
intentional (announcements are member-only content) or an oversight. Given
the generic name `broadcast` (not `broadcastToMembers`), I lean toward
oversight, but didn't change it without more product context — this is
exactly the kind of call that needs a real answer from whoever owns the
feature, not a guess.

---

## 6. 🔧 `classes.cancel` never refunds credits

**Location:** `src/server/routers/classes.ts`, `cancel` procedure

**Cause:** When an admin cancels an entire class, every `"booked"` row is
set to `"cancelled"` with no refund logic at all — unlike
`bookings.cancel`, which refunds credits when cancelled ≥12 hours ahead.

**Impact:** A member who loses a class because staff cancelled it is
treated worse than one who cancels their own booking last-minute.

**Decision: recommended fix, not yet applied.** Low risk — the refund
logic already exists in `bookings.cancel` and just needs to be applied per
affected booking inside the loop. Left as documented-only due to time;
would be the next priority fix if more time becomes available.

---

## 7. 🔧 `reschedule` skips waitlist promotion

**Location:** `src/server/routers/reschedules.ts`, `reschedule` procedure

**Cause:** The original booking is cancelled with a plain
`update(bookings).set({status: "cancelled"...})`, bypassing the
promotion logic that exists in `bookings.cancel`.

**Impact:** Rescheduling out of a full class frees a real seat, but no
one on the waitlist is promoted into it — the seat sits invisible until
someone books it directly.

**Decision: recommended fix, not yet applied.** Same promotion block used
in fix #2 could be reused here. Left as documented-only due to time.

---

## 8. 📝 `plans.subscribe` allows duplicate active memberships

**Location:** `src/server/routers/plans.ts`, `subscribe` procedure

**Cause:** No check for an existing active membership before inserting a
new one.

**Impact:** A member who subscribes twice ends up with two active
membership rows, each with a separate credit pool — one of which can
become unreachable, since `activeMembershipFor()` only ever picks the one
with the latest `endDate`. A paying customer can silently lose access to
credits they paid for.

**Decision: documented, not fixed.** The correct fix requires a product
decision (block the second subscription outright, vs. "stack" it by
extending the existing membership) that needs more context than the code
alone provides.

---

## 9. 📝 `members.profile` selects a membership with no status filter

**Location:** `src/server/routers/members.ts`, `profile` procedure

**Cause:** Picks the membership with the latest `endDate` regardless of
`status` — unlike `bookings.activeMembershipFor()`, which requires
`status === "active" AND endDate >= today`.

**Impact:** A cancelled or expired membership can display on a member's
own profile page as if it were current.

**Decision: documented, not fixed.** Straightforward fix would be reusing
`activeMembershipFor()` here instead of a separate looser query — flagged
as the next priority alongside #6/#7 if time allows.

---

## 10. 📝 `payments.refund` doesn't touch credits or existing bookings

**Location:** `src/server/routers/payments.ts`, `refund` procedure

**Cause:** Cancels the associated membership's status but never adjusts
`creditsRemaining` or looks at bookings already made using that
membership's credits.

**Impact:** A member could be refunded, have their membership cancelled,
and still hold an active booking paid for with credits from that
now-cancelled membership.

**Decision: documented, not fixed.** Genuinely ambiguous — leaving
existing bookings intact after a refund may be the intended,
customer-friendly behavior. Needs a policy answer, not a code guess.

---

## 11. 📝 Corporate check-ins can't be traced to a specific booking

**Location:** `src/db/schema.ts` (`checkins.bookingId`) and
`corporate-bookings.ts` `markAttended` (inserts `bookingId: null`)

**Cause:** `checkins.bookingId` is a foreign key only to `bookings`, with
no equivalent column for `corporateBookings`.

**Impact:** Corporate check-ins are recorded with only `userId` and a
timestamp — not traceable to which specific class booking they came from.   

**Decision: documented, not fixed.** Requires a schema migration
(e.g. a nullable `corporateBookingId` column), which is a higher-risk
change than the time remaining allows for safely.

---

## 12. 📝 Expired sessions are never cleaned up

**Location:** `src/server/routers/auth.ts` / `src/server/trpc.ts`

**Cause:** `login` inserts new session rows on every login; nothing ever
deletes expired ones. `createContext` correctly rejects expired sessions
(`expiresAt > now`), so this is not a correctness bug — just unbounded
table growth over time.

**Impact:** None functionally; the `sessions` table grows indefinitely.

**Decision: documented, not fixed.** Out of scope — adding a cleanup job
for a demo-scale SQLite app isn't warranted in this timeframe.

---

## 13. 🔧 Repeated role checks in `trainers.ts`

**Location:** `src/server/routers/trainers.ts` — repeated in
`upcomingClasses`, `availability`, `setAvailability`, `removeAvailability`

**Cause:** The same manual check is written out four times:
```ts
if (ctx.user.role !== "trainer") {
  throw new TRPCError({ code: "FORBIDDEN", message: "Only trainers can access this." });
}
```
instead of using a shared procedure, the way `staffProcedure` and
`adminProcedure` already do in `trpc.ts`.

**Recommended fix (not yet applied):**
```ts
// Add to trpc.ts, following the existing pattern:
export const trainerProcedure = protectedProcedure.use(({ ctx, next }) => {
  if (ctx.user.role !== "trainer") {
    throw new TRPCError({ code: "FORBIDDEN", message: "Trainers only." });
  }
  return next({ ctx });
});
```
Then replace `protectedProcedure` + the manual check in all four
`trainers.ts` procedures with `trainerProcedure`. Pure refactor, no
behavior change — safe to apply if time allows.

---

## 14. 📝 Repeated company lookup-or-404 in `admin-companies.ts`

**Location:** repeated in `getById`, `updateActive`, `topUp`, `linkMember`

**Cause:** Same select-then-404 block written out four times instead of a
shared helper.

**Decision: documented, not fixed.** Recommended fix is a
`getCompanyOrThrow(db, id)` helper in `src/lib/` — straightforward but
deprioritized below the zero-risk fixes given remaining time.

---

## 15. 📝 `bookings.ts` and `corporateBookings.ts` are ~90% duplicate files

**Location:** the two full router files

**Cause:** Nearly identical logic (book, cancel, mark attended, roster)
with `memberships` swapped for `companies`. Even `hoursUntil()` is
redefined identically in both.

**Decision: documented, not fixed.** The highest-risk refactor on this
list — touches the most business-critical code in the app. Attempting a
full unification without enough time left to re-test both flows
thoroughly would be riskier than leaving the duplication in place and
documenting it clearly, which is the choice made here.

---


